  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/0/0b/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_12.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Peter drops by the <i>Daily Bugle</i> office to complain about his coverage and notes that Betty has returned as Jameson's secretary.&nbsp; The <i>Bugle</i> accuses Spider-Man of allowing Dr. Octopus to escape in Philadelphia (last issue).&nbsp; Now Ock is on a crime spree \"thruout [sic] the nation.\"&nbsp; Ock is <i>trying</i> to goad Spider-Man into attacking him, <i>certain</i> that he's more powerful.&nbsp; But when Spider-Man doesn't immediately come after him, Ock decides to return to NYC and find <i>him</i> instead!\n\nOck has no way of knowing that the <i>reason</i> Spider-Man hasn't hunted him down is because, at that moment, Peter Parker is at home and seems close to catching a cold.&nbsp; (Plus, he can't afford the fare to get out to where Dr. Octopus is, not to mention final exams are coming up soon.)\n\nAt the Bugle, Betty answers the phone, not realizing that it's Ock, only calling to ensure she's there.&nbsp; He'll use <i>her</i> as bait for Spider-Man.&nbsp; And indeed he <i>does</i> arrive for her, just after Parker comes to the office to walk Betty home.&nbsp; Ock demands that Jameson print a note in the paper, telling Spider-Man to meet him at Coney Island!&nbsp; If he wants the girl, he must come <i>alone</i>, but he'll allow one photographer to take pictures of the defeat.&nbsp; Jameson turns to Parker and tags him for the job!\n\nPeter is indeed getting woozy from the oncoming illness, and it's affecting his ability to cling to the walls, but nothing will stop him from getting to Coney Island!&nbsp; But he's obviously feeling very weak, but he lunges at Ock, trying to throw a punch, but the blow is barely felt by his opponent, who thinks it's a trick!&nbsp; Ock is easily able to handle the weakened Spider-Man and even says \"You're like a human punching bag!!&nbsp; What's <i>happened</i> to you?\"&nbsp; and \"He doesn't even struggle as I try to remove his mask!&nbsp; There can only be <i>one </i>answer...\"\n\nAnd he rips off Spider-Man's mask!&nbsp; \"I should have <i>known</i>!!&nbsp; It <i>isn't</i> Spider-Man!&nbsp; It's that weakling brat, Peter Parker!\"&nbsp; The onlookers are amazed!&nbsp; Peter could have been killed!&nbsp; The police get him home, where hours later, Aunt May has summoned the doctor, who assures her that it's only the \"twenty-four hour virus\" and that he'll be fine by morning.\n\nAnd sure enough, when he wakes up, he's good as new.&nbsp;  At school, his classmates have all heard that he impersonated Spider-Man to try and rescue Betty, for which Liz thinks he's wonderful, but Flash is sure Peter only did it because he was sure he wouldn't actually run into Ock.&nbsp; Liz tells him off!\n\nMeanwhile, Ock is furious that the papers are saying he was fooled by a teenager and allowed his hostage to go free even <i>without</i> having to fight Spider-Man!&nbsp; He releases the large animals from the zoo to terrorize the city.&nbsp; That occupies the police and Spider-Man while he continues his rampage.\n\nFinally, Ock dislodges a sign on the side of a building, hoping that threatening the citizens below will finally draw out the web-slinger.&nbsp; It does, and with that, the battle begins!\n\n<b>CONTINUE HERE</b>\n\n\nPages of battle action ensue, with Spider-Man pointing out \"You're not fighting a weak <i>imitation</i> of Spider-Man now...this time you're facing the <i>real thing</i>!\"&nbsp; Eventually the two crash into a \"deserted sculptor's studio\" where some spilled cleaning fluid starts a fire (Ock points out that \"this building is deserted\" - man, I'm now wondering whether NYC is littered with abandoned buildings - <i>wow</i>).&nbsp; Spider-Man points out that they both need to get out of there, but Ock is livid enough to pay no heed and proceeds to get stuck under a fallen sculpture while Spider-Man makes a web shield to protect him against the flames and makes it to an open window and escapes.&nbsp; Soon the fire department arrives and drags the weakened Doc Ock out into the hands of the waiting police.\n\nOn the street below, Peter Parker now watches and turns down a request from Liz Allen (apparently misspelled in this issue - actually Allan) to attend a party, but he's got a date with Betty - she can have Flash instead!&nbsp; Liz realizes that she probably deserves that treatment after the way they've treated him.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | CatchingACold | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\nPeter mentions he doesn't have any money to go after Doc Ock, even if he <i>was</i> healthy.&nbsp; (I guess he must have used all his available cash when he took the plane to Philadelphia during last month's adventure.)\n\nIn the last issue, Doc Ock escaped after the speed boat he and Spider-Man were on crashed into a piling near Philadelphia.\n\n[ASSET: right | HowDoesHeKnow | width=13%] Dr. Octopus' plan to kidnap Betty in order to entice Spider-Man to chase after him is a sound enough plan (and he did, after all, try the same thing on page 18 of last month's issue).&nbsp; We also know that Betty Brant was the one who picked up Ock from prison (see its page 4).\n[BR]\n[BR] But how on Earth did Ock know that Betty had worked at the <i>Daily Bugle</i>?\n[BR] Or that she had <i>returned</i> to that job now?\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(We must assume that they chatted during their car ride from NYC to Philly.)]\n\n\n<b>What about Peter's bum ankle?&nbsp; Is there any mention of it?</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: left | TooEasy | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: center | Unmasked | width=large]\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: right | ZillionBucksAgain | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n(<i>Great panel on the bottom right of page 9</i>!)\n\n\n[ASSET: left | LizAllanWakesUp | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n\n\"Average\" is about the word to use here.&nbsp; The novelty of seeing Peter unmasked by an enemy was sure to sell copies of the book, and indeed it wasn't a trick (sort of) -- but while Ock does indeed remove the mask, he thinks <i>he's</i> the one being tricked.&nbsp; The gimmick of having Peter be too sick to fight Ock felt sort of like a trick, but okay, it could happen...\n\nSay what you want about the unmasking:\n[HANG] * Peter was sick, so no one believed he was really Spider-Man\n[HANG] * the fact that it <i>sort of</i> felt like a cheat in the end\n\nBut you can't argue that a cover like that <i>had</i> to have elicited sales!\n[BR] Especially one that went out of its way to say [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Hey everyone!&nbsp; This is no <i>trick!</i>  This is <i>for real</i>!\"]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=In other words, the gimmick <b><i>worked</i></b>!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And you have to at <i>least</i> tip your cap to Marvel for that!)]\n\n\n<b>The whole bit with the release of the large zoo animals serves little purpose.&nbsp; Ock did it to draw out Spider-Man, which was fine in and of itself, but it largely just served the purpose of burning through around three story pages that featured little more than the title hero battling lions and gorillas.&nbsp; Pardon me, but that's <i>hardly</i> compelling stuff!</b>\n\n[ASSET: center | LeeDitSign | width=large]\nI laughed on page 14 when Doc Ock dislodged a sign on the side of the building that read \"LEEDIT Inc\" -- a painfully obvious amalgamation of the names \"Lee\" and \"Ditko\" -- funny!&nbsp; In retrospect, I suppose it <i>could</i> have also been \"LEEDIT <b>Co</b>\" (short for \"Company\" instead of \"Incorporated\"), but perhaps that might have been a little <i>too</i> blatant!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Still, it would have been even <i>funnier</i> in my opinion...)]\n\n\n\nThe supporting characters are mostly relegated to the back seat in this story, as the action takes center stage, with the one possible ongoing change being the attitude that Liz Allan has of him after realizing that he put himself in danger and \"posed\" as Spider-Man to try and rescue Betty.&nbsp; Is this a one-issue attitude adjustment, or is she really seeing Peter in another light?&nbsp; We'll stay tuned!\n\n<b>But I noticed that Aunt May and the kids at school seemed mostly to be \"inserted\" - they didn't add much, making their appearances appear to be more perfunctory than necessary.&nbsp; I noted in my review of <i>last</i> month's issue that it wasn't necessary that they all show up, as long as the ones that <i>do</i> serve a purpose in the story.&nbsp; That seems to have been \"violated\" here.</b>\n\n\nAs for Doc Ock, the action sequences were good but otherwise not really noteworthy.&nbsp; Spider-Man catches the villain, he is captured, and life goes on.&nbsp; It was, in the end, only an average issue with no notable <i>faults</i>, mind you, but not life-altering in any way and probably soon to be forgotten about.\n\nI loved seeing the \"LEEDIT Inc\" sign, as I pointed out above, and also the cover blurb of \"Not a Dream!&nbsp; Not an Imaginary Tale!\" -- obviously in response to DC's common practice of the time.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Note that the cover of <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> #121 has a similar blurb, which is probably why I immediately took note of it on the cover of <i>this</i> issue!&nbsp; I suppose X-Men #42 does as well.]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>FIXME</b>",
  "side_note": [
    "The <b>Special Announcements Section</b> of this issue [LINK:1964:ASM_12|ASM #12|2027-06-02] explains that Betty is actually <i>younger</i> than Peter, having had to leave school and get a job because she needed the money, intending to complete her education when she gets a chance.&nbsp; By the way, that announcement goes on to declare Peter as a \"high school senior.\")"
  ],
  "letters_page": "The \"Spider's Web\" page contains a letter from future comic book creator Dave Cockrum.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}